{
  "$comment": "Bone contract for the shared armature: `humanoid` maps UnityEngine.HumanBodyBones names to the bone names in the exported FBX, `extra` lists the non-humanoid bones with their parents. Blender validates the armature against it on export; Unity configures the humanoid rig from it by name. The FBX rest pose is the T-pose. An extra bone with `twist` is a twist helper: Unity gives it a rotation constraint that follows `source` about the bone's own long axis at `weight` (the body base's own setup). An extra bone with `physics` is the root of a chain; the value names the PhysBone settings Unity gives it (see CassPrefabs). A chain root with `tile` carries only what is on that UV tile (uv-tiles.json): its physics runs while the tile is shown, and a build that cuts the tile out of the mesh leaves the physics out with it. A chain root with `pcOnly` has physics that Android and iOS have no room for: those builds leave it out and the chain's bones stay as they rest on their root. An extra bone with `follow` is turned by a rotation constraint: it takes `weight` of however far `source` has turned from rest, both measured against their shared parent, so what hangs from it is carried part of the way with a limb. `weightAlone` is the share it takes instead in a build that leaves its physics out, where nothing else keeps the limb from coming through. `colliders` lists the capsules that keep chains off the body: each lies along `bone` from its joint to the joint of `to`, is `radius` metres thick, and pushes the chains whose `physics` it names.",
